WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:04.020
Disney movies are designed to be rewatched
over and over again.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.549
When you really dig into them, though, you
might notice that they often end with a few

00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:12.559
dangling and intriguing questions that are
never fully answered, from nonsensical world-building

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.040
to some very uncomfortable romantic implications.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:19.660
Even if you've never seen Disney's Aladdin,
you probably know how the whole Genie thing

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:20.160
works.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.920
Once the title character's big blue buddy
is released from the lamp, he tells Aladdin

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.980
that he will grant any three wishes, with
only a simple trio of limitations.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.740
"I can't kill anybody so don’t ask.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.980
Rule number 2: I can’t make anybody fall
in love with anybody else."

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.620
His final rule is that he can't resurrect
the dead, but then goes on to say that it's

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.380
not impossible, it's just that he doesn't
like to do it.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.120
Not only does that imply that he's done it
before, doesn't it mean that Genie could break

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.710
the rules if he wanted, and he just chooses
not to?

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:51.860
There's some evidence later in the film that
the Genie has a lot more wiggle room than

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:52.640
he is letting on.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.740
Aladdin's first wish is to be a prince, but
the Genie never really grants this wish at

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:57.240
all.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.560
Aladdin doesn't get a kingdom to rule or in
his case, a principality.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.880
His "Prince Ali" shtick is merely a ruse,

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:04.420
“I beg your pardon?”

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:05.020
“Your ruse.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:06.640
Your cunning attempt to trick me.”

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.630
and when that ruse is discovered, it turns
out that royal credentials weren't really

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:12.850
necessary for a marriage to Jasmine.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:17.240
When Jafar wishes to be Sultan, he actually
gets to rule Agrabah, but all Aladdin gets

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:17.820
is a parade.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.000
At one point, the Genie tells Aladdin that
he can't save Aladdin's life unless Aladdin

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.740
uses a wish, but at another point, Al tricks
the genie into granting a wish for free, because

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.950
he never technically uses the words "I wish."

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:33.920
That means the Genie can use his phenomenal
cosmic powers even outside of actual wish-granting.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.799
We never learn for certain how much flexibility
the Genie has in wish granting, but those

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.920
three rules certainly aren't the whole story.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.720
101 Dalmatians is a certified canine classic,
but there's a seemingly vital question at

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.220
the heart of the film that it never really
bothers to answer.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.880
Why is Cruella de Vil hanging around with
Roger and Anita to begin with?

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:56.120
If you have a vague recollection that maybe
Cruella was Anita's boss or something, you're

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:56.620
wrong.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.200
That's how it works in the 1996 live-action
remake, where Anita is a fashion designer

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:06.329
working for Cruella, but in the original 1961
film, she's just an old friend from Anita's

00:02:06.329 --> 00:02:07.060
school days.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:10.860
The two characters seem to hate everything
about each other, to the point where Roger

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:15.150
and Anita straight up write a mean song about
how much she sucks, so what is either of them

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:16.540
getting out of this friendship?

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:20.560
One advantage of living in the pre-Facebook
era of the '60s was that once you got out

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.340
of school, you never had to keep in touch
with your old classmates and their criticisms

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:25.340
of your life choices.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:30.620
"Anita, don't be ridiculous, you can’t possibly
afford to keep them, you can scarcely afford

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:31.440
to feed yourselves."

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.840
Anita's life seems like the result of some
weird Faustian bargain.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.820
On the one hand, you will have a hundred adorable
puppies and never have to worry about money

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:43.030
again, but on the other hand, that one person
you hated most from high school will be hanging

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:46.680
around your house all the time, and you can
never ever get rid of them.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:51.220
When Buzz Lightyear first arrives in Andy's
room in Toy Story, there's something different

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:51.720
about him.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.720
Unlike the other toys, who are happy with
their roles as Andy's playthings, Buzz doesn't

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.260
think of himself as a toy at all.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:02.560
He thinks he's the real Buzz Lightyear, stranded
on an alien planet after crashing his "spaceship,"

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.260
which is actually just the box he came in.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.740
For most of the movie, this is handled pretty
consistently.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:10.620
For instance, Buzz doesn't want to open his
helmet, in case the atmosphere is toxic.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.800
He thinks he has a real laser gun in his arm.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:16.240
He even believes he can fly, which turns out
to be pretty dangerous..

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:19.720
"YOU ARE A TOY!"

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:23.460
But if Buzz really believes that he's a Space
Ranger, why does he freeze when humans are

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:23.960
around?

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:28.860
Fixing his spaceship seems to be beyond Buzz's
capabilities, so why not make contact with

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.980
this friendly giant to get help fixing his
spaceship?

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.840
It might just be that Buzz thinks it's a good
idea to do what the native life forms of this

00:03:34.849 --> 00:03:39.640
strange bedroom planet do whenever a creature
roughly a hundred times their size walks in,

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:42.010
just in case Andy's not quite as friendly
as he seems.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:46.430
Or maybe, somewhere in his tiny plastic chest,
Buzz knows the truth all along.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:50.840
He feels an instinctual need to freeze around
humans, but can't understand why he's doing

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:53.200
it, and perhaps even makes excuses for it
later.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.620
It's a shame that the movie never explores
this angle, because we would have loved to

00:03:56.630 --> 00:04:00.970
see Buzz deal with the cognitive dissonance
of not believing he is a toy, while on another

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:03.360
level not being able to resist behaving like
one.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:08.080
Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs may contain
a magic mirror, a shapeshifting evil queen,

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.980
and a princesses who talks to animals, but
the most fantastical thing in this setting

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.070
has to be the strange work ethic of the seven
dwarfs.

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:18.579
In "Heigh Ho," the dwarfs sing about their
work in the mine, claiming that they're swinging

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:23.850
their pickaxes for 12-hour shifts and digging
up literal cart-loads of diamonds, rubies,

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.300
and other precious gemstones.

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:28.980
Given this, you'd imagine they'd be pretty
wealthy, and yet they're practically living

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:32.590
on top of each other in a single shoddy cabin
with one bedroom.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:36.260
One of the other things they mention in the
song is that they enjoy what they do, so maybe

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:38.220
the dwarfs are just working for the joy of
work.

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:42.400
After all, when they finish up at the end
of each day, they just toss their gems into

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.500
a vault, lock the door, and then hang up the
key on a hook... right outside the door.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.080
This could imply that they just don't care.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.800
Alternatively, the dwarfs simply might not
know that diamonds are valuable, which might

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:55.980
be why we see Dopey literally throwing sub-standard
rubies in the trash.

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:59.660
Their house is a mess, and there's an entire
song about how they don't know how to clean

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:03.120
themselves, so it's safe to assume that no
one ever taught them about finances.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.440
Or maybe there's some unseen boss.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:08.940
Someone gave them their shoddy house and taught
them how to build elaborate glass coffins

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:11.500
in exchange for working in the mine all day
for free.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.919
As presented in the film, the plot of Monsters,
Inc. is mostly comedy.

00:05:15.919 --> 00:05:20.660
A little girl from the human world nicknamed
Boo ends up in the fantastical city of Monstropolis,

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:24.980
becoming the pet of two monsters named Mike
and Sully, who are more afraid of her than

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:25.560
she is of them.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.960
This seems like a recipe for family-friendly
fish out of water antics, and that's mostly

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:32.760
what we get, from the perspective of our main
characters.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:36.530
However, if the viewpoint had stayed in the
human world, the film would have quickly switched

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:39.479
genres once Boo's parents discovered she was
missing.

00:05:39.479 --> 00:05:43.710
There's basically an entire second movie here,
a crime thriller complete with crying parents,

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:49.400
police reports, search parties, and possibly
even nationwide media coverage after Boo vanishes

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:51.789
with no explanation into a parallel dimension.

00:05:51.789 --> 00:05:54.720
Perhaps even more interesting, however, would
be what happens next.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.490
When Boo returns to her bedroom in the middle
of the night as mysteriously as she vanished,

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:01.190
what would be the reaction of her family and
their community?

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:03.910
Would Boo's family now be accused of faking
the whole thing?

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:07.330
Would her parents be able to return to life
as it once was, or would they suspect the

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:09.260
truth about the monsters that live in the
closet?

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:13.580
At the end of Monsters, Inc., when Sully returns
to Boo's room so that he can visit her again,

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:16.370
is Boo's mom waiting just off-screen with
a baseball bat?

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:17.980
Now that's a sequel we'd like to see.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.750
Of all the romances in Disney films, there
are few more purely adorable than The Lion

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:24.819
King's pairing of Simba and Nala.

00:06:24.819 --> 00:06:28.121
If you want to keep thinking about it that
way, maybe skip ahead a little bit, because

00:06:28.121 --> 00:06:30.840
things are about to get real uncomfortable
real quick.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.750
Okay, are all the normal people gone?

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:33.640
Just the weirdos left?

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:37.700
Good, because here's a news flash: Simba and
Nala are probably related.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:40.320
Let's start with their mothers, Sarabi and
Sarafina.

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:43.880
For real lions, all the lionesses in a pride
are typically related.

00:06:43.889 --> 00:06:47.660
If that's the case here, then Simba and Nala
are probably at least second cousins.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:50.069
It gets even worse when you think about their
dads.

00:06:50.069 --> 00:06:53.910
Again, in a real lion pride, all the cubs
are typically offspring of the same two or

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:54.680
three males.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:57.820
In The Lion King, there are two males: Mufasa
and Scar.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:02.020
Since the entire plot of the movie revolves
around Scar and Mufasa being brothers, the

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:06.380
best Simba and Nala can hope for is cousins,
and they might be as close as half-siblings.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:09.250
This whole line-of-succession thing just got
way more complicated.

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:12.340
"Long live the king."

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:17.160
Once upon a time, the world of The Incredibles
was full of superheroes, but then the government

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:18.360
forced them all to retire.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:22.410
Now they are monitored by the National Supers
Agency, which both makes sure that they aren't

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:26.560
using their powers in public, and also helps
relocate them if their identities are ever

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:27.060
exposed.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:31.080
One hero who has gotten to know the Agency
quite well is Bob Parr, formerly known as

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:32.120
Mr. Incredible.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:36.080
Because he can't resist the siren song of
returning to superheroics every now and then,

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:40.750
Parr and his family have been relocated and
given new identities by the Agency multiple

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:41.240
times.

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:45.900
Eventually, Parr is given an opportunity to
feel like a hero again when a mysterious billionaire

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:48.120
hires him to disable his renegade robot.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:51.610
It later turns out that this robot isn't a
renegade at all.

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:55.849
Parr's mysterious boss built it, and others
like it, for the sole purpose of killing superheroes,

00:07:55.849 --> 00:08:00.500
and it's already racked up a body count of
fifteen supers before it goes after the Parrs.

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:05.940
So, quick question: if the only job of an
entire government agency is to monitor retired

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:09.710
superheroes, did nobody notice that 15 of
them had gone missing?

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:13.330
Seriously, if you lose track of a guy who
can shoot lasers out of his eyes, that's troubling

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:17.379
for a whole lot of reasons, and if more retired
heroes start vanishing, you should really

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:18.640
start letting people know.

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:22.980
Then again, given how frequently Bob's has
to be relocated, maybe someone in the Agency

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:26.900
figured that Bob having a mysterious disappearance
wouldn't be the end of the world.

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:29.400
At least it would cut down on the amount of
paperwork they had to do.

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:32.400
Some Disney movies raise a few questions.

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:36.130
Beauty and the Beast raises so many that we
don't even know where to begin.

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:38.920
Why does no one in Belle's town know about
the Beast's castle?

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:41.300
Wasn't he just their ruler a few years ago?

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:43.380
Is everything in the castle a former person?

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:46.120
We see the all of the dishes and even the
silverware dancing.

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:47.680
Did they used to be people?

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:50.260
If so, did the castle not have spoons before?

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:55.380
The Beast is 20 years old (a fact that's central
to the plot), and Lumiere says during "Be

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:57.399
Our Guest" that they've been cursed for ten
years.

00:08:57.399 --> 00:09:00.170
Does that mean the prince was just ten when
he was cursed?

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:03.040
How much of a jerk do you have to be to put
a curse on a ten-year-old?

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:04.440
And why curse his servants?

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:05.680
They didn't do anything wrong.

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:07.820
Speaking of ages, how old is Chip?

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:09.100
He seems younger than ten.

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:11.040
Did he stop maturing when he was cursed?

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:14.820
Would he actually be a teenager now if he
wasn't a cup, or did Mrs. Potts give birth

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:16.250
to him while she was a teapot?

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:17.670
If so, who's his father?

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:19.320
Can the furniture-people have kids?!

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:20.740
"This is impossible"

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:23.820
"I know it is… but here we are."

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:28.680
Okay, to be fair, the 2017 remake tried to
answer a few of these questions, but in some

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:31.820
ways, that resulted in a movie that was way
talkier and less fun.

00:09:31.870 --> 00:09:36.440
The original Beauty and the Beast is great,
and Disney movies in general are great, in

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:38.900
part because they have so many weird loose
threads like this.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:43.880
After all, after you watch a movie a hundred
times, isn't it nice to still have a few questions?

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.170
